 // change the page, then run python3 tools/build-pages.py.
 
 // Each page as Markdown, by address.
 export const PAGES = {
+  "/stock": "# Add your records\n\nYOUR DAILY RECORDS\n\n---\n\nThis is the Markdown version of https://findash.tech/stock.\n\nFinDash: [Home](https://findash.tech/) · [Dashboard](https://findash.tech/dashboard) · [Help](https://findash.tech/help) · [Waitlist](https://findash.tech/waitlist) · [Developers](https://findash.tech/developers) · [About](https://findash.tech/about) · [Contact](https://findash.tech/contact) · [Privacy](https://findash.tech/privacy) · [Terms](https://findash.tech/terms) · [llms.txt](https://findash.tech/llms.txt) · [Sitemap](https://findash.tech/sitemap.xml)\n",
   "/": "# Free profit and loss (P&L) dashboard for small businesses\n\nEveryone can manage finances Built for *non‑tech*\n\nRevenue, costs and budget from the figures you already have. Copy your monthly totals from QuickBooks, Xero or your own spreadsheet into our template, or snap a photo of a supplier invoice, drop it in, and see the month. Spreadsheets are read and totalled in your own browser.\n\nExample No formulas, no pivot tables, just your monthly totals in one simple template.\n\n[Open dashboard](https://findash.tech/dashboard) [Log in](https://findash.tech/login) [See sample figures](https://findash.tech/#preview)\n\nNo signup. Your spreadsheet never leaves your browser.\n\nBuilt for owners who read the numbers themselves\n\nEvery cost line itemised One month at a glance Budget vs. actual\n\nFrom the [demo dashboard](https://findash.tech/dashboard) · September 2026\n\n## Profit, costs and budget vs actual, month by month\n\nRevenue\n\n**$33,000** Down 48.4% on August\n\nNet margin\n\n**−11.0%** A loss: costs ran past revenue\n\nTotal costs\n\n**$36,640** Across 4 cost groups\n\nBudget used\n\n**107.8%** $2,640 over budget\n\nForecast example\n\n## See where it's heading, not just where it's been\n\nFinDash draws the trend through your monthly profit. In the demo it turned down in August, a month before September's loss.\n\nNet profit (demo) Direction (next 3 months)\n\n*Line chart of the demo's net profit: 7,580 in July, 5,420 in August and a 3,640 loss in September, with a dashed line showing the trend continuing down through December*\n\nWith fewer than six months on file you see the direction only. From six months, FinDash projects the next three in dollars, with a range.\n\n## Who it's built for\n\nNo jargon\n\n### Non-tech owners\n\nPlain labels and a ? beside every term. If you can copy a total from your Profit and Loss, you can use this.\n\nYour currency\n\n### International teams\n\nSee your figures in euros, pounds, dollars and more. Amounts written 1.250,00 or 1,250.00 are both read correctly.\n\nOne file\n\n### Freelancers\n\nOne spreadsheet, a row per month, or photos of your invoices. Every month at a glance.\n\nZero setup\n\n### Small teams\n\nNo account needed, no onboarding call. Your figures stay in your browser, or log in to have them on any computer.\n\nComing next\n\n## Inventory from photos, in your language\n\nWe're building inventory counts from photos of your shelves, and FinDash in more languages for kitchens and suppliers that don't work in English. Tell us about your food business and you'll hear first.\n\n[Join the waitlist](https://findash.tech/waitlist)\n\nAbout\n\n## What is FinDash?\n\nFinDash (findash.tech) is a free web app that turns a small business's monthly figures, from an Excel or CSV spreadsheet or a photo of a supplier invoice, into a profit and loss (P&L) dashboard of revenue, costs, profit and budget vs actual, with plain-language AI advice. It is not accounting software.\n\n- **Profit and loss, month by month:** revenue, 14 cost lines in four groups, gross profit, net profit and net margin.\n- **Budget vs actual:** how much of each month's spending budget you used, and where you went over.\n- **What needs attention:** losses, budget overruns and unusual costs, read straight off your figures.\n- **Where it's heading:** a trend through your net profit, and advice written by Claude when you ask for it.\n\nRunning a restaurant? The [restaurant P&L dashboard](https://findash.tech/restaurant-profit-and-loss) page shows food cost, labor and prime cost, month by month.\n\nQuestions\n\n## Questions people ask\n\n**Is there a free profit and loss dashboard for small businesses?**\n\nYes. FinDash is free, and the dashboard works without an account. Add a spreadsheet of your monthly totals to see each month's revenue, costs, gross and net profit, and how much of your budget you used.\n\n**Do I need QuickBooks or Xero to use FinDash?**\n\nNo. Fill in FinDash's Excel or CSV template, or use your own layout: columns are matched by name, and a sheet with the months across the top is turned the right way round. If you use QuickBooks or Xero, copy each month's totals from your Profit and Loss report.\n\n**Is my spreadsheet uploaded anywhere?**\n\nNo. Your spreadsheet is read and added up in your own browser. Only the things you ask for that need a server leave your computer: reading an invoice photo, writing advice, or saving your figures to a free account. The privacy policy lists exactly what is sent.\n\n**Can FinDash read a photo of a supplier invoice or receipt?**\n\nYes, with a free account. Claude, an AI model, reads every line; FinDash checks the arithmetic against the printed total; and you confirm the lines before anything is added to your month.\n\n**What does budget used mean?**\n\nYour total costs as a share of the spending budget you set for the month. Over 100% means you spent more than you planned, and the month shows up under What needs attention.\n\n**What is the difference between gross profit and net profit?**\n\nGross profit is revenue minus direct costs: cost of goods sold, labor, employee meals and payroll tax. Net profit is what is left after every cost, and net margin is net profit as a share of revenue.\n\n**Does FinDash show cash flow?**\n\nNo. It shows profit: each month's revenue minus its costs. Cash can differ, for example when customers pay late or you buy stock ahead, so a profitable month can still leave you short of cash.\n\n**Does it work for restaurants, shops and trades?**\n\nYes. FinDash was built for a restaurant, and its four cost groups work just as well for shops, trades, service businesses and freelancers. Figures can be in dollars, euros, pounds and other currencies.\n\n**Is FinDash accounting software?**\n\nNo. It doesn't connect to your bank, keep your books, send invoices, take payments, run payroll or file taxes. It shows the figures you already have, clearly, so you can act on them.\n\n---\n\nThis is the Markdown version of https://findash.tech/.\n\nFinDash: [Home](https://findash.tech/) · [Dashboard](https://findash.tech/dashboard) · [Help](https://findash.tech/help) · [Waitlist](https://findash.tech/waitlist) · [Developers](https://findash.tech/developers) · [About](https://findash.tech/about) · [Contact](https://findash.tech/contact) · [Privacy](https://findash.tech/privacy) · [Terms](https://findash.tech/terms) · [llms.txt](https://findash.tech/llms.txt) · [Sitemap](https://findash.tech/sitemap.xml)\n",
-  "/dashboard": "# Profit and loss (P&L) dashboard\n\n**Demo data** You are looking at sample figures. Add your own to see your business.\n\nYour spreadsheet stays on your computer. A photo is sent to Claude to be read. Your figures only leave when you press **Get advice**. [How we handle your data](https://findash.tech/help#privacy)\n\n1. **Get the template**\n\n   One row per month: revenue, budget and 14 cost lines. It comes filled in with the sample months so you can see the layout.\n\n2. **Fill in your numbers**\n\n   Replace the sample rows with your own monthly totals from QuickBooks, Xero or your own spreadsheet. For costs you can also add photos of supplier invoices and receipts, once you're logged in.\n\n   **How to get your numbers**\n\n   QuickBooks Online\n\n   1. Go to **Reports** and open **Profit and Loss**.\n   2. Pick the months you want and set **Display columns by** to **Months**.\n   3. Copy each month's income and cost totals into the matching template columns.\n\n   Xero\n\n   1. Open **Reports**, then **Profit and Loss**.\n   2. Choose your date range and compare by month.\n   3. Copy each month's totals into the template.\n\n   Your own spreadsheet\n\n   1. Put one month per row, using the template's column names.\n   2. Column order doesn't matter, and common names like Wages or Advertising are recognised.\n\n   **Budget** is the most you planned to spend that month, not a sales target. Leave it blank if you don't set one. A cost that has no column of its own? Add it to the closest one; [what each cost group means](https://findash.tech/help#glossary).\n\n3. **Add the file**\n\n   Press **Choose file** or drag it onto this page. Excel (.xlsx) or CSV, or a photo of an invoice (with a free account). Your figures are saved in this browser, so you don't have to add them again.\n\nDrag your file here, or click to choose it\n\nExcel (.xlsx) or CSV · one file with every month · or a photo of an invoice or receipt\n\n## Your month at a glance\n\nRevenue, costs, profit and budget vs actual for each month.\n\n---\n\nThis is the Markdown version of https://findash.tech/dashboard.\n\nFinDash: [Home](https://findash.tech/) · [Dashboard](https://findash.tech/dashboard) · [Help](https://findash.tech/help) · [Waitlist](https://findash.tech/waitlist) · [Developers](https://findash.tech/developers) · [About](https://findash.tech/about) · [Contact](https://findash.tech/contact) · [Privacy](https://findash.tech/privacy) · [Terms](https://findash.tech/terms) · [llms.txt](https://findash.tech/llms.txt) · [Sitemap](https://findash.tech/sitemap.xml)\n",
+  "/dashboard": "# Profit and loss (P&L) dashboard\n\n**Demo data** You are looking at sample figures. Add your own to see your business.\n\nYour spreadsheet stays on your computer. A photo is sent to Claude to be read. Your figures only leave when you press **Get advice**. [How we handle your data](https://findash.tech/help#privacy)\n\n1. **Get the template**\n\n   One row per month: revenue, budget and 14 cost lines. It comes filled in with the sample months so you can see the layout.\n\n2. **Fill in your numbers**\n\n   Replace the sample rows with your own monthly totals from QuickBooks, Xero or your own spreadsheet. For costs you can also add photos of supplier invoices and receipts, once you're logged in.\n\n   **How to get your numbers**\n\n   **QuickBooks Online**\n\n   1. Go to **Reports** and open **Profit and Loss**.\n   2. Pick the months you want and set **Display columns by** to **Months**.\n   3. Copy each month's income and cost totals into the matching template columns.\n\n   **Xero**\n\n   1. Open **Reports**, then **Profit and Loss**.\n   2. Choose your date range and compare by month.\n   3. Copy each month's totals into the template.\n\n   **Your own spreadsheet**\n\n   1. Put one month per row, using the template's column names.\n   2. Column order doesn't matter, and common names like Wages or Advertising are recognised.\n\n   **Budget** is the most you planned to spend that month, not a sales target. Leave it blank if you don't set one. A cost that has no column of its own? Add it to the closest one; [what each cost group means](https://findash.tech/help#glossary).\n\n3. **Add the file**\n\n   Press **Choose file** or drag it onto this page. Excel (.xlsx) or CSV, or a photo of an invoice (with a free account). Your figures are saved in this browser, so you don't have to add them again.\n\nDrag your file here, or click to choose it\n\nExcel (.xlsx) or CSV · one file with every month · or a photo of an invoice or receipt\n\n## Your month at a glance\n\nRevenue, costs, profit and budget vs actual for each month.\n\n---\n\nThis is the Markdown version of https://findash.tech/dashboard.\n\nFinDash: [Home](https://findash.tech/) · [Dashboard](https://findash.tech/dashboard) · [Help](https://findash.tech/help) · [Waitlist](https://findash.tech/waitlist) · [Developers](https://findash.tech/developers) · [About](https://findash.tech/about) · [Contact](https://findash.tech/contact) · [Privacy](https://findash.tech/privacy) · [Terms](https://findash.tech/terms) · [llms.txt](https://findash.tech/llms.txt) · [Sitemap](https://findash.tech/sitemap.xml)\n",
   "/restaurant-profit-and-loss": "# Free restaurant profit and loss (P&L) dashboard\n\nSee your restaurant's food cost, labor and prime cost as a share of sales, with rent, net margin and budget vs actual, month by month. Free, it reads your spreadsheet in your own browser, and no signup is needed.\n\n[Open the dashboard](https://findash.tech/dashboard)\n\n## What it shows for a restaurant\n\nFinDash was first built for a restaurant, so its cost lines are a restaurant's: food and drink, labor, payroll tax, staff meals, packaging, card fees, rent and the rest. Each month you see:\n\n- **Food cost:** cost of goods sold (food and drink) as a share of sales.\n- **Labor:** wages, payroll tax and staff meals as a share of sales.\n- **Prime cost:** food cost plus labor, the biggest share of most restaurants' spending and the part you can change week to week. In FinDash it is the **direct costs** group.\n- **Rent and other fixed costs:** what doesn't shrink when sales do.\n- **Net profit and net margin:** what is left after every cost.\n- **Budget vs actual:** how much of the month's spending budget you used, and where you went over.\n- **What needs attention:** loss-making months, budget overruns and unusual costs.\n\n## An example: three months at the demo restaurant\n\nThese are the demo figures the [dashboard](https://findash.tech/dashboard) opens on.\n\n| Month | Sales | Food cost | Labor | Prime cost | Rent | Net margin |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| July 2026 | $72,000 | 36.1% | 27.1% | 63.2% | 8.3% | 10.5% |\n| August 2026 | $64,000 | 32.8% | 25.8% | 58.6% | 9.7% | 8.5% |\n| September 2026 | $33,000 | 33.3% | 27.9% | 61.2% | 18.2% | −11.0% |\n\nIn September sales roughly halved. Prime cost stayed near 61% of sales, because food and staff costs came down with them. Rent didn't: the same $6,000 went from 8.3% of sales to 18.2%, and the month ended $3,640 down. Fixed costs are what turn a slow month into a loss, and that is the first thing the dashboard flags.\n\n## How your restaurant P&L lines map to FinDash\n\nCopy each month's totals into the template's matching column. A cost without its own line goes in the one that behaves most like it.\n\n| On your P&L | FinDash line | Group |\n|---|---|---|\n| Food and beverage purchases | Cost of goods sold | Direct costs (prime cost) |\n| Wages and salaries | Labor | Direct costs (prime cost) |\n| Payroll taxes | Payroll tax | Direct costs (prime cost) |\n| Staff meals | Employee meals | Direct costs (prime cost) |\n| Takeout containers, bags | Packaging | Operating costs |\n| Card processing, delivery-app commissions | Credit card fees | Operating costs |\n| Advertising, promotions | Marketing | Operating costs |\n| POS terminals, small equipment | Hardware | Operating costs |\n| Gas, electricity, water | Utilities | Variable overhead |\n| Repairs, cleaning, pest control | Maintenance | Variable overhead |\n| Rent | Rent | Fixed overhead |\n| Insurance | Insurance | Fixed overhead |\n| Licenses and permits | Business license | Fixed overhead |\n| Phone and internet | Telephone | Fixed overhead |\n\n## Restaurant benchmarks, as a rough guide\n\nFigures quoted across the industry, which vary a lot by concept and location. Your own trend, month by month, tells you more than any single target.\n\n- **Prime cost:** often targeted at about 60–65% of sales for full-service restaurants, and a little lower for quick-service ([7shifts](https://7shifts.com/blog/restaurant-prime-cost-guide/); [Chron](https://smallbusiness.chron.com/common-food-labor-cost-percentages-14700.html)).\n- **Food cost:** commonly quoted at about 25–35% of sales, and **labor** at about 25–35%, higher for table service ([Chron](https://smallbusiness.chron.com/common-food-labor-cost-percentages-14700.html)).\n- **Rent and occupancy:** a common rule of thumb keeps it under about 8–10% of sales ([Restaurant Business](https://restaurantbusinessonline.com/advice-guy/how-figure-out-prime-cost-occupancy-percentages)). Because rent is a fixed amount, the share swings with sales, as September shows.\n- **Net margin:** thin. Full-service restaurants often keep only a few percent of sales as profit, around 3–5% by many estimates ([Bloom Intelligence](https://bloomintelligence.com/blog/restaurant-profit-margins/)).\n\n## Get your restaurant's numbers in\n\n1. On the [dashboard](https://findash.tech/dashboard), press **Download Excel template** (or CSV).\n2. Fill in one row per month: sales, your budget, and the cost lines above. Copy the totals from your Profit and Loss report in QuickBooks or Xero, or from your own spreadsheet.\n3. Drop the file on the dashboard. It is read in your browser; nothing is uploaded.\n4. With a free account, add photos of supplier invoices too: each line is read, checked against the total, and added to its month once you confirm it.\n\n[Open the dashboard](https://findash.tech/dashboard)\n\n## Questions restaurant owners ask\n\n**What is prime cost?**\n\nPrime cost is your cost of goods sold (food and drink) plus your total labor: wages, payroll tax and staff meals. It is usually the largest part of a restaurant's spending. FinDash shows it each month as the direct costs group, as a share of sales.\n\n**What is a good prime cost for a restaurant?**\n\nFull-service restaurants often aim for about 60 to 65% of sales, and quick-service a little lower. Much above that leaves little room for rent and everything else. How your own prime cost moves from month to month matters more than any one target.\n\n**What is a good food cost percentage?**\n\nFood cost is commonly quoted at about 25 to 35% of sales, depending on the menu and the concept. FinDash shows it each month as cost of goods sold divided by sales.\n\n**What is a good net profit margin for a restaurant?**\n\nRestaurant margins are thin: full-service restaurants often keep around 3 to 5% of sales as net profit, and a slow month can easily turn into a loss when rent and other fixed costs stay the same.\n\n**Can I use the numbers from my POS or accounting software?**\n\nYes. Copy each month's totals from your Profit and Loss report in QuickBooks or Xero, or from your POS sales reports, into FinDash's template. FinDash doesn't connect to your POS or accounting software; it reads the spreadsheet you give it.\n\n**Does FinDash replace my bookkeeper or accountant?**\n\nNo. FinDash doesn't keep your books, run payroll or file taxes. It turns the monthly totals you already have into a clear picture of food cost, labor, prime cost and profit, so you can see where the month went.\n\n---\n\nThis is the Markdown version of https://findash.tech/restaurant-profit-and-loss.\n\nFinDash: [Home](https://findash.tech/) · [Dashboard](https://findash.tech/dashboard) · [Help](https://findash.tech/help) · [Waitlist](https://findash.tech/waitlist) · [Developers](https://findash.tech/developers) · [About](https://findash.tech/about) · [Contact](https://findash.tech/contact) · [Privacy](https://findash.tech/privacy) · [Terms](https://findash.tech/terms) · [llms.txt](https://findash.tech/llms.txt) · [Sitemap](https://findash.tech/sitemap.xml)\n",
   "/help": "# Help\n\nHow to get your numbers in, what each figure means, and exactly what happens to your data.\n\n- [Get your numbers in](https://findash.tech/help#get-started)\n- [If a file won't load](https://findash.tech/help#problems)\n- [What the numbers mean](https://findash.tech/help#glossary)\n- [How we handle your data](https://findash.tech/help#privacy)\n- [Printing and sharing](https://findash.tech/help#sharing)\n- [Contact](https://findash.tech/help#contact)\n\n## Get your numbers in: Excel, CSV or an invoice photo\n\nRunning a restaurant? The [restaurant P&L dashboard](https://findash.tech/restaurant-profit-and-loss) page shows how your lines map to FinDash's, and what food cost, labor and prime cost to expect.\n\nFinDash needs one spreadsheet with a row for each month. The quickest way is the template: on the [dashboard](https://findash.tech/dashboard), press **Download Excel template** (or CSV). It comes filled in with three sample months, so you can see what goes where.\n\n1. **Month and Year:** the month's name, such as July, and the year, such as 2026.\n2. **Revenue:** everything that came in from sales that month.\n3. **Budget:** the most you planned to spend that month in total. It is a spending limit, not a sales target. Leave it blank if you don't set one.\n4. **The 14 cost columns:** each month's total for that cost. Leave a cost at 0 if you don't have it.\n\nSave the file and drop it on the dashboard, or press **Choose file**. Excel (.xlsx) and CSV both work.\n\n### From QuickBooks Online\n\n1. Go to **Reports** and open **Profit and Loss**.\n2. Pick the months you want and set **Display columns by** to **Months**.\n3. Copy each month's income and cost totals into the matching template columns.\n\n### From Xero\n\n1. Open **Reports**, then **Profit and Loss**.\n2. Choose your date range and compare by month.\n3. Copy each month's totals into the template.\n\n### From your own spreadsheet\n\n- Put one month per row, with the template's column names across the top. The order of the columns doesn't matter.\n- Common alternative names are recognised: Wages or Payroll for Labor, Advertising for Marketing, Cost of goods for Cost of goods sold, Repairs for Maintenance, and so on.\n- A sheet laid out the other way round, with costs down the side and months across the top, is read too.\n- Rows that add other rows up (Total costs, Gross profit, Net income) are spotted and left out, so nothing is counted twice.\n\nA cost that doesn't have its own column? Add it to the closest one; the [cost groups below](https://findash.tech/help#glossary) say what belongs where.\n\nThe older two-file layout (one file with month, month num, revenue and budget, and one with the expense columns) still works: add both files together.\n\n### From a photo of an invoice or receipt\n\nFor costs, you can skip typing: take a photo of a supplier invoice, a bill or a receipt, and add it to the dashboard the same way as a file. A screenshot of an emailed invoice works too.\n\n- **Take a clear photo.** The whole page, flat, in good light, with every amount in the frame.\n- **Claude reads it.** Each charged line is read, given in English (invoices in two languages are fine), and put in the cost line it belongs to: food and ingredients go to Cost of goods sold, takeout containers and bags to Packaging, and so on. Handwritten notes are not counted as charges, and free items are counted at zero.\n- **The arithmetic is checked.** Each line's quantity times price must match its amount, and the lines must add up to the printed total. If they don't, the photo is read a second time with the mistakes pointed out. An amount that was cut off or too faded to read is worked out from quantity times price and marked so you can check it.\n- **You check it before anything is added.** You see every line, can change the cost line any of them goes to, and choose the month (the invoice's own date by default). Nothing is added until you press **Add**.\n- **It adds to that month.** The invoice's costs are added on top of the month's figures, so only add invoices your monthly file doesn't already count. A receipt for money you took in, such as a till report, adds to revenue. Every invoice you add is listed under the import box, with a **Remove** button.\n- **It lists what you buy.** At the bottom of the dashboard, **What you buy** gathers every item from the invoices you've added: how much came in, what it cost in all, and whether its price went up or down since the delivery before. The same item on two invoices is matched by the supplier's item code, so it helps to keep the codes in the photo.\n\nPDF invoices can't be read yet: open the PDF and take a screenshot of it instead.\n\n## If a file won't load\n\n- **\"Columns not recognised\"**: The file needs a Revenue column and at least one cost column such as Rent or Cost of goods sold. Check the top row holds column names, or start from the template.\n- **\".xls is the older Excel format\"**: Open the file in Excel and choose **Save As**, then **Excel Workbook (.xlsx)** or **CSV**.\n- **\"Could not open\"**: The file may be damaged or password-protected. Remove the password, save it again, and retry.\n- **\"Need one more file\"**: You added half of the older two-file layout. Add the other file, or switch to the one-file template.\n- **The months are in the wrong order or show the wrong year**: Add a Year column. Without one, every month is assumed to be in the current year.\n- **\"Photos can't be read here\"**: Reading photos needs this site's AI service, and the copy of FinDash you're using doesn't have it set up. Use the template instead.\n- **\"This browser can't open that kind of photo\"**: iPhones often save photos as HEIC, which some browsers can't open. Take a screenshot of the photo, or change your camera setting to \"Most Compatible\", and add that instead.\n- **\"The lines add up to … but the printed total is …\"**: Even after a second look, the reading doesn't match the invoice's total. Check the lines marked in red against your paper copy. Retaking the photo flatter and in better light usually fixes it.\n- **A figure is 100 times too big or too small**: Write amounts in full. 1,250.00 and 1.250,00 are both read correctly, but a short \"1,25\" could mean either 1.25 or 125, so it is read as 125.\n\n## What the numbers mean\n\nEvery cost sits in exactly one of four groups, so the groups add up to your total costs and revenue minus total costs is your net profit. The same explanations appear behind each **?** on the dashboard.\n\n### The four cost groups\n\n- **Direct costs**: What it costs to make each sale or do each job: stock and ingredients (cost of goods sold), staff wages, staff meals and payroll tax.\n- **Operating costs**: Costs that grow when you are busier but are not tied to one sale: packaging, card fees, marketing and small equipment.\n- **Variable overhead**: Running costs that go up when you are busier, but not in step with sales: utilities and maintenance.\n- **Fixed overhead**: Costs that don't change with sales, like rent, insurance, licences and phone. Usually the hardest to cut, because most of it is under contract.\n- **Other (uncategorised)**: Money counted in this group's total that did not match one of the named lines, usually a column in your file with a name we did not recognise.\n\n### Results\n\n- **Revenue**: Everything that came in from sales in the period, before any costs are taken off.\n- **Total costs**: Every cost in the four groups added together.\n- **Gross profit**: Revenue minus direct costs, before rent, marketing and the other running costs.\n- **Net profit**: What is left of revenue after every cost. A negative number is a loss.\n- **Net margin**: The share of revenue you keep after every cost. 10% means you keep 10 of every 100 that comes in.\n- **Shortfall**: How far costs ran past revenue in a month that made a loss.\n- **% of revenue**: Each line as a share of revenue, so a busy month and a quiet one can be compared fairly.\n\n### Budget\n\n- **Budget**: The most you planned to spend in total that month. It is a spending limit, not a sales target.\n- **Budget used**: Total costs as a share of your budget. Over 100% means you spent more than you planned.\n- **Typical month**: Average total costs across every month on file.\n- **Swing**: How much higher the worst month was than the average. “Steady” means within 10%.\n\n### Forecast\n\n- **Trend**: A straight line drawn through your monthly net profit and carried forward. It shows the direction things are moving, not a promise.\n- **Range**: How far past months strayed from the trend. A wider range means the months have been less predictable.\n\n## How we handle your data\n\n**Your spreadsheet stays on your computer.** It is opened and added up by your own browser. It is never uploaded. Without an account, FinDash keeps none of your figures anywhere but your own browser.\n\nNine things can leave your computer, and only these:\n\n- **When you log in or create an account:** your email and password go to Supabase, the service FinDash uses for logins, to check who you are. Accounts are optional.\n- **While you are logged in:** your worked-out monthly figures and the invoice lines you confirmed are saved to your account with Supabase, so they are there on any computer you log in on. Not your file, not your photos, and nobody else can read them. **Forget my data** removes them from your account too.\n- **When you add a photo** (while logged in): the photo is sent to Claude, Anthropic's AI model, to be read. FinDash does not store it; only the lines you confirm are kept.\n- **When you press Get advice** (while logged in): each month's totals (revenue, the four cost groups, net profit and budget) are sent to Claude, Anthropic's AI model, which writes the advice. No line items, no names, not your file. FinDash does not store them.\n- **When a column name isn't recognised** (while logged in): the column names from your file, never the numbers, are sent so the unfamiliar ones can be matched to the right cost group. Files that use the template's names never need this.\n- **If you join the waitlist:** your email and your answers about your business are saved with Supabase, so we can email you about FinDash and decide what to build first. Never your figures.\n- **If a forecasting model is offered:** once you have six months on file, a button may appear under the forecast. Pressing it sends the net profit figure for each month, nothing else.\n- **If something on the page breaks:** the error's technical message and which part of FinDash it came from are sent to FinDash's own server log, so it can be fixed. Never your figures or your file.\n- **Each page you visit:** which page it was, the site that linked to it, and your browser, device type and country are counted by Vercel Web Analytics, without cookies, so we can see how FinDash is used. Never your figures or your file.\n\n**Saved in this browser:** after you add a file or a photo, the worked-out monthly figures, the invoice lines you confirmed and your latest advice (not the files or photos themselves) are kept in this browser on this device, so they are there next time. Unless you are logged in, they are never sent anywhere. Press **Forget my data** on the dashboard to remove them. On a shared computer, do that before you leave.\n\n**If you log in:** the browser keeps a login cookie that only FinDash's own server can read, not the page or anything on it. It lasts up to 30 days, or until you press **Log out**. Logging out also removes your figures from that browser; they stay safe in your account.\n\n**If this browser and your account disagree** (say you added a file before logging in), the dashboard asks which set of figures to keep, rather than guessing.\n\n**Why photos and advice need an account:** each one is a paid request to Claude, so each account has a daily allowance. Everything else on the dashboard works without one.\n\n**To delete your account:** log in, open the [Log in](https://findash.tech/login) page and press **Delete my account**. Your saved figures are removed straight away, and an email asking for your login to be deleted opens for you to send; you'll get a reply once it's done.\n\nAll of this, and who processes your data for us, is in the [privacy policy](https://findash.tech/privacy).\n\n## Printing and sharing\n\n- **Print or save as PDF** gives a clean, light report of the period on screen. In the print window, choose **Save as PDF** as the printer to get a file to email.\n- **Export to Excel** downloads every line for every month side by side, with a total column, ready for your accountant, partner or bank.\n- **Print count sheet**, under **What you buy**, prints a sheet for counting what's on your shelves: the food, ingredients and packaging from your invoices, under the names and codes your supplier uses, with a box beside each to write how many are on hand. It can also be downloaded for Excel.\n\n## Contact\n\nFound a problem, or have a question this page doesn't answer? Email [support.findash@findash.tech](mailto:support.findash@findash.tech?subject=FinDash). Please don't include your figures.\n\n---\n\nThis is the Markdown version of https://findash.tech/help.\n\nFinDash: [Home](https://findash.tech/) · [Dashboard](https://findash.tech/dashboard) · [Help](https://findash.tech/help) · [Waitlist](https://findash.tech/waitlist) · [Developers](https://findash.tech/developers) · [About](https://findash.tech/about) · [Contact](https://findash.tech/contact) · [Privacy](https://findash.tech/privacy) · [Terms](https://findash.tech/terms) · [llms.txt](https://findash.tech/llms.txt) · [Sitemap](https://findash.tech/sitemap.xml)\n",
   "/login": "# Log in to FinDash\n\nAccount\n\nWelcome back. Log in with the email you signed up with.\n\n## About accounts\n\n- **Optional** The dashboard works without an account. Reading invoice photos and getting advice need one, since each is a paid request.\n- **Just your email and a password** Kept by Supabase, the service FinDash uses for logins. Your password is never stored by FinDash.\n- **Your figures on any computer** While you're logged in, your monthly figures and confirmed invoice lines are saved to your account. Never your spreadsheet or photos.\n\nJust want to see your numbers? [Open the dashboard](https://findash.tech/dashboard) without an account.\n\n---\n\nThis is the Markdown version of https://findash.tech/login.\n\nFinDash: [Home](https://findash.tech/) · [Dashboard](https://findash.tech/dashboard) · [Help](https://findash.tech/help) · [Waitlist](https://findash.tech/waitlist) · [Developers](https://findash.tech/developers) · [About](https://findash.tech/about) · [Contact](https://findash.tech/contact) · [Privacy](https://findash.tech/privacy) · [Terms](https://findash.tech/terms) · [llms.txt](https://findash.tech/llms.txt) · [Sitemap](https://findash.tech/sitemap.xml)\n",
